reement that we've  seen with  89 for illumina uh for categorical  agreement versus 88 for nanopore so very  comparable uh but we also evidently saw  better performance with our  carbopenemies producing organisms at 95  and 96 categorical agreement  and it was slightly lower with our are  significantly lower for our  non-carbopytamine producers and that's  because it could be more difficult to  predict carbapenem resistance in the  absence of that acquired gene of  acquired resistance gene but we have  been working  along with our heiress collaborators to  further uh tweak  the ability to define um carbapenem  resistance among those known  carbopenemies producers  the other thing that comes up is that  we're now starting to see treatment  guidelines based on whether mechanisms  of resistance testing is performed or  not and these treatment guidelines uh  recommend therapy  the recommended therapy will differ  based off of the mechanism mediating  resistance so for example if your  phenotype is third generation  cephalosporin resistance  you don't know based off of the  phenotype whether that's mediated by  esbl or app c that being said if you  knew the mechanism mediating resistance  um the recommended treatment would  differ where with espl meropenem would  be  the recommended therapy versus absi  cefine so not only understanding the  phenotype but knowing the mechanism  needing mediating that phenotype can be  helpful and the same with carbapenemies  producers were based off of whether it's  a  producer or not and the genotype the  recommended therapy will vary so we've  been working with eris again to try to  merge the predictive ast and that  genotypic  information to try to more accurately  guide therapy  and  we recently put this data together um  looking at the combination of predictive  ast and genotypic information among a  group of enterobacteriaceae so expanding  past the klebsiella pneumonia that we  had focused on before and applying it to  e coli other